1. Service Management

    sudo systemctl status firewalld - Check firewall status
    
    sudo systemctl start firewalld - Start firewall service
    
    sudo systemctl enable firewalld - Enable auto-start on boot

2. Zone Management

    sudo firewall-cmd --get-zones - List all available zones
    
    sudo firewall-cmd --set-default-zone=public - Change default zone
    
    sudo firewall-cmd --zone=public --list-all - View rules for specific zone

3. Service & Port Management

    sudo firewall-cmd --zone=public --add-service=http --permanent - Allow HTTP service
    
    sudo firewall-cmd --zone=public --add-port=8080/tcp --permanent - Allow custom port
    
    sudo firewall-cmd --zone=public --remove-service=http --permanent - Remove service
    
    sudo firewall-cmd --zone=public --remove-port=8080/tcp --permanent - Remove port

4. Advanced Configuration

    sudo firewall-cmd --zone=public --add-masquerade --permanent - Enable NAT masquerading
    
    sudo firewall-cmd --reload - Apply all configuration changes

5. Rich Rules (Advanced)

    Use --add-rich-rule and --remove-rich-rule for complex filtering rules

Intrusion Detection/Prevention (Suricata)
1. Installation

    sudo dnf install suricata - Install Suricata IDS/IPS

2. Configuration

    sudo nano /etc/suricata/suricata.yaml - Edit main configuration file
    
    Key Settings:
    
    Network interface to monitor
    
    Rule file locations (/etc/suricata/rules/)
    
    Logging directories
    
    Alert thresholds

3. Rule Management

    sudo suricata-update - Download and update threat rules

    Rule Sources: Emerging Threats (ET), Snort rulesets

4. Service Management

    sudo systemctl start suricata - Start Suricata in IDS mode
    
    sudo systemctl enable suricata - Enable auto-start

5. Monitoring & Logs

    cat /var/log/suricata/fast.log - View security alerts
    
    /var/log/suricata/ - Main log directory

6. IPS Mode

    Edit suricata.yaml to enable prevention mode
    
    Caution: Can block legitimate traffic if misconfigured

7. Maintenance

    Regular rule updates
    
    Fine-tune rules to reduce false positives
    
    Continuous monitoring and response planning

OpenVPN Server Setup
1. Installation

    sudo dnf install openvpn - Install OpenVPN package

2. Server Configuration

    sudo mkdir /etc/openvpn/server - Create server directory
    
    sudo cp /usr/share/doc/openvpn/sample/sample-config-files/server.conf /etc/openvpn/server/ - Copy sample config
    
    sudo nano /etc/openvpn/server/server.conf - Edit server configuration

3. Key Configuration Parameters

    bash
    port 1194
    proto udp
    dev tun
    server 10.8.0.0 255.255.255.0
    push "redirect-gateway def1 bypass-dhcp"
    push "dhcp-option DNS 8.8.8.8"
4. Certificate Generation

    cd /etc/openvpn/server - Navigate to server directory
    
    sudo openssl dhparam -out dh.pem 2048 - Generate DH parameters
    
    sudo openssl req -new -newkey rsa:2048 -days 365 -nodes -x509 -keyout ca.key -out ca.crt - Generate CA
    
    sudo openssl req -new -newkey rsa:2048 -days 365 -nodes -keyout server.key -out server.crt - Generate server cert

5. Network Configuration

    sudo nano /etc/sysctl.conf - Enable IP forwarding
    
    Add: net.ipv4.ip_forward = 1
    
    sudo sysctl -p - Apply kernel changes

6. Firewall & NAT Configuration

    sudo firewall-cmd --permanent --add-port=1194/udp - Allow OpenVPN port
    
    sudo firewall-cmd --permanent --add-masquerade - Enable NAT for VPN clients
    
    sudo firewall-cmd --reload - Apply firewall changes

7. Service Management

    sudo systemctl start openvpn@server - Start OpenVPN service
    
    sudo systemctl enable openvpn@server - Enable auto-start

8. Client Setup

    Generate client certificates using easy-rsa
    
    Create client configuration file (.ovpn)
    
    Transfer certs and config to client securely
    
    Connect using: openvpn --config client-config.ovpn
